The figure shows that seismic onset times following the Mw 6.4 event are **highly variable in space and time**, with **distinct clusters of immediate and delayed activation**. Neither along-strike position nor distance to the Mw 7.1 epicenter exhibits a simple monotonic relationship with onset time. In particular, **the earliest post-Mw 6.4 onsets occur predominantly at intermediate distance from Mw 7.1 rather than nearest to it**, while regions closest to Mw 7.1 commonly activate later. This pattern is more consistent with **heterogeneous fault-segment response and delayed cascade triggering** than with a simple propagating front toward the eventual Mw 7.1 hypocenter.[0m

